--- MESSAGE ---
One resolution, and it is draco's rather than the JVM's

A type name resolved to a definition by `getResourceAsStream`, which hands back
whichever copy the classloader reached first. That is the JVM's answer to a question
draco has to answer for itself: a definition path must mean the same thing projected
into Scala, Haskell or TypeScript, and those three shadow by different rules. Keeping
first-wins would guarantee the divergence.

So resolution enumerates instead of searching. `definitionSources` returns EVERY root
carrying the path (getResources, plural) and `loadFromResource` requires exactly one:
none is still a stub — DomainBuilder.isStub depends on that and a named-but-unauthored
member must keep loading empty — one reads, and two or more is an error naming the
sources. Order carries no meaning, because order is precisely what cannot be preserved
across hosts. Another target enumerates its own roots and applies the same rule, so the
targets agree by construction rather than by coincidence.

GENERATOR'S DUPLICATE LOADER IS GONE, and this is what made the change worth anything:
half the system was still resolving first-wins through Generator's own copy — its own
resource read, its own path construction, its own tryLoad. The two had already drifted.
Generator built `/$np/$name.json` unconditionally, so a type with no package resolved
to `//Name.json` where TypeName.resourcePath gives `/Name.json`. Nothing caught it
because nothing in the corpus has an empty package — a divergence waiting for the first
type that did. Two loaders cannot both be the definition path.

Prerequisite was #37, and not in principle: unique-or-error compares TypeNames that
came from separate parses at separate roots, so nothing makes them the same object.
Under reference equality the collision check would have reported "no collision", always.

The suite passed unchanged, which also says no TypeName currently resolves to two
resources on the classpath. That was the failure worth having, and it did not fire.

NEXT, and not yet done: the roots are still implicit — whatever the host classloader
happens to expose. Making them an explicit set is what turns this into a DefinitionPath
proper, and it is the step where "read definitions embedded in runtime libraries" stops
being a JVM property and becomes a draco one.
